      "id": 1,
      "text": "Charleston was one of the few Southern cities that resisted little during the Civil Rights era, welcoming integration early.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charleston%2C_Arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Belle Museum and Chapel, founded by lifelong residents Delbert and Mary Belle Ervin, preserves Charleston’s family histories and artifacts.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.oocities.org/bellemuseumandchapel/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A railroad that ran through Charleston for nearly a century is now remembered by a bench from the old depot in the museum’s railroad exhibit.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.oocities.org/bellemuseumandchapel/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1954, Charleston’s school board voted to disband the segregated school and admit black students, opening the doors to 11 black children in August.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charleston%2C_Arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Julia Singleton Cormack, the first female teacher in Charleston’s school system, is highlighted among the museum’s early school history displays.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
